WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.451
XML is still common in production .

2
00:00:02.451 --> 00:00:03.852
NET systems.

3
00:00:03.852 --> 00:00:16.459
It appears in APIs, uploaded files, configuration, SOAP and legacy service integrations, document formats, partner exchanges, feeds, identity workflows, and queue-based processing.

4
00:00:16.459 --> 00:00:27.595
Even when a new application is mostly JSON-based, XML often remains in the edges of the environment where old systems, vendors, and document workflows connect.

5
00:00:27.595 --> 00:00:31.728
The security issue is that XML is not just a string format.

6
00:00:31.728 --> 00:00:44.895
XML processors can support DTD processing, external references, schema validation, XPath queries, XSLT transformations, digital signatures, namespaces, encodings, and rich error reporting.

7
00:00:44.895 --> 00:00:54.490
Those features are useful in the right context, but they also mean XML processing is an application behavior that deserves design review.

8
00:00:54.490 --> 00:00:58.903
The actual risk depends on several conditions working together.

9
00:00:58.903 --> 00:01:00.864
Where did the XML come from.

10
00:01:00.864 --> 00:01:02.334
Which parser is used.

11
00:01:02.334 --> 00:01:04.226
Which features are enabled.

12
00:01:04.226 --> 00:01:06.957
Can the parser read external resources.

13
00:01:06.957 --> 00:01:13.120
Can the input influence query expressions, schema locations, or transformation behavior.

14
00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:15.362
What happens when parsing fails.

15
00:01:15.362 --> 00:01:19.284
What does the application trust after parsing completes.

16
00:01:19.284 --> 00:01:22.926
These questions matter more than the file extension.

17
00:01:22.926 --> 00:01:36.934
A practical default is to treat XML from users, partners, vendors, uploads, queues, email, object storage, and external services as untrusted unless there is a strong, verified reason to do otherwise.

18
00:01:36.934 --> 00:01:49.891
Signed or authenticated transport may identify a sender, but the document still needs parser controls, structure validation, and business validation before the application relies on it.

19
00:01:49.891 --> 00:01:53.883
A secure XML baseline makes the processing path explicit.

20
00:01:53.883 --> 00:02:07.821
It defines parser settings, resource limits, trusted schema sources, safe query patterns, restricted transformation behavior, careful signature handling, safe error handling, and operational logging.

21
00:02:07.821 --> 00:02:09.782
The goal is not to fear XML.

22
00:02:09.782 --> 00:02:14.685
The goal is to make XML behavior visible, constrained, and reviewable.
